media: sun50i-h713-hdmirx: do not switch the source on a republished descriptor

Since 0117 the driver publishes the VidDec record again when the source
geometry changes, and the firmware answers by rebuilding its whole window
chain. Read out of the firmware's own log at level 5, that first rebuild is
completely correct for 1280x720 -> 1920x1080:

    capture_cfg  [1280,720][1280,720]   display_cfg [1920,1080][1920,1080]
    CapWinNode   ratio 1:1, rowbyte 80, line_num 720
    ProcWinNode  in [1280,720] out [1920,1080]  ratio [43690 x 43690]
    PanelWinNode out [0,0,1920,1080]

Then this driver destroys it. Publication clears the INCAP output enable, this
re-arm answers with a source switch, and the switch makes the firmware run
SetSignalInfo -> SetCaptureCfg -> SetDisplayCfg a second time. That pass loses
the geometry: the capture comes out 2/3 short, 853x480 instead of 1280x720,
0x06940928 reads 0x1E0 and the rowbyte 0x36. The wall shows a smeared band.
Cycling 1080p/720p a second time leaves the capture wedged entirely.

The switch is right for the FIRST publication -- a single SetSource does not
bring the capture back there, the switch away and back does, after 536..544 ms
(doku/86 section 4). It is not needed for a later one: poking a descriptor and
pointing the slot registers at it drives the firmware's own chain all the way
to memory_agent ENABLE, device-verified 2026-06-13
(re/notes/color-csc-blocker.md), and memory_agent_onoff's mask 0x008 is exactly
this enable bit.

So on a republication, wait for the firmware first. Keep the switch as the
fallback: a capture that never returns is worse than a geometry that is wrong,
and this way the log says which of the two happened -- either "Firmware hat
selbst freigegeben" or the warning that the fallback had to run.

